A.append(dict(
slug="dental-fillings-explained",
title="Dental Fillings Explained: Materials, the Procedure and Aftercare",
tag="Procedures",
desc="How cavities are found and filled, the differences between composite, amalgam, glass ionomer and indirect fillings, what the appointment feels like, and aftercare tips.",
blurb="Tooth-coloured, silver or ceramic? How a filling is placed and what affects the choice.",
alt="A cutaway of a molar with a small cavity being filled, next to a curing light glowing blue",
body="""
<p>A filling is the most common way to repair a tooth damaged by decay. The idea is simple: remove the decayed part of the tooth, then rebuild the missing shape with a durable material so you can chew normally and bacteria cannot move deeper. The details, though, from how the cavity is found to which material is used, are worth understanding before you sit down for one.</p>

<h2>How a cavity forms and is found</h2>
<p>Bacteria in plaque feed on sugars and starches and produce acids. Repeated acid attacks draw minerals out of enamel. At first this shows up as a chalky white spot, which can sometimes be reversed with fluoride and better habits. If demineralisation continues, the surface breaks down and a cavity forms. Once decay reaches the softer dentin layer underneath enamel, it can spread faster, and eventually it can approach the pulp, the living tissue at the centre of the tooth.</p>
<p>Dentists find cavities by looking at and feeling the tooth surfaces, and by using bitewing X-rays, which show decay between back teeth that is invisible to the eye. Small cavities do not always hurt, which is why regular checkups catch many before you notice anything. Early lesions confined to enamel are sometimes monitored rather than filled; ask your dentist whether that is an option for you.</p>

<h2>Filling materials compared</h2>
<p>There is no single "best" filling material. The right choice depends on where the tooth is, how big the cavity is, how much biting force it takes, your risk of new decay, cost and coverage, and your own preferences.</p>

<figure class="table-scroll">
<table>
<caption>Common filling materials at a glance</caption>
<thead><tr><th>Material</th><th>Look</th><th>Typical strengths</th><th>Things to know</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Composite resin</td><td>Tooth-coloured</td><td>Bonds to tooth, conservative preparation, good for front and many back teeth</td><td>Technique-sensitive; needs a dry field; may stain or wear over time</td></tr>
<tr><td>Amalgam ("silver")</td><td>Metallic grey</td><td>Long track record, durable under chewing forces, tolerant of moisture</td><td>Contains mercury bound in an alloy; use has declined and some countries restrict it; the FDA has issued advice for certain higher-risk groups</td></tr>
<tr><td>Glass ionomer</td><td>Tooth-coloured, less glossy</td><td>Releases fluoride; useful near the gumline and in baby teeth</td><td>Generally less wear-resistant than composite on biting surfaces</td></tr>
<tr><td>Inlays and onlays (ceramic or gold)</td><td>Tooth-coloured or gold</td><td>Made outside the mouth for larger repairs; very durable</td><td>Usually two visits or in-office milling; higher cost</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>

<p>Amalgam fillings that are intact and not decayed underneath do not usually need to be replaced just because of the material. Removing a sound filling also removes healthy tooth structure. If you are concerned, discuss it with your dentist, who can explain current guidance and the trade-offs.</p>

<h2>What happens during the appointment</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Numbing.</strong> The dentist usually numbs the area with a local anaesthetic, first applying a numbing gel so the injection is more comfortable. Very small or shallow fillings are sometimes done without it.</li>
<li><strong>Removing decay.</strong> A dental drill or other instrument removes the decayed tooth structure. Some dentists use a rubber dam, a thin sheet that isolates the tooth and keeps it dry.</li>
<li><strong>Shaping.</strong> The cavity is shaped to hold the chosen material. For composite, the tooth is etched and a bonding agent applied.</li>
<li><strong>Placing the material.</strong> Composite is placed in layers, each hardened with a blue curing light. Amalgam is packed in and carved while soft.</li>
<li><strong>Checking the bite.</strong> You bite on thin coloured paper so the dentist can find and smooth any high spots.</li>
<li><strong>Polishing.</strong> The surface is smoothed so it feels natural against your tongue.</li>
</ol>
<p>A straightforward filling often takes somewhere between 20 minutes and an hour, depending on size and how many teeth are being treated.</p>

<h2>After your filling</h2>
<p>Your lip, tongue and cheek may stay numb for a few hours. Avoid hot drinks and chewing until the numbness wears off so you do not bite or burn yourself. Composite fillings are fully hardened when you leave; with amalgam, dentists often suggest avoiding hard chewing on that side for the rest of the day.</p>
<p>Some sensitivity to cold, pressure or sweets for a few days, or even a couple of weeks, is common, particularly with deeper fillings. It should gradually improve. Call your dentist if:</p>
<ul class="checklist">
<li>Your bite feels high or the tooth hurts sharply every time you close.</li>
<li>Sensitivity is getting worse rather than better.</li>
<li>You develop a throbbing ache, pain that wakes you at night, or swelling.</li>
<li>The filling feels rough, cracked or loose, or food keeps catching.</li>
</ul>
<p>A high bite is an easy fix: the dentist smooths it in a short visit. Worsening pain can mean the nerve is inflamed, which occasionally happens with deep cavities and may need further treatment.</p>

<h2>How long fillings last</h2>
<p>Fillings do not last forever. Their lifespan depends on the material, the size of the filling, how hard you bite or grind, and how well the edges are kept free of new decay. Large fillings in back teeth take a lot of stress. When a filling replaces a big share of a tooth, your dentist may suggest an onlay or a crown instead because the remaining walls of the tooth are at risk of cracking.</p>
<p>The best way to make any filling last is to prevent new decay around it: fluoride toothpaste twice a day, daily cleaning between teeth, fewer sugary snacks and drinks between meals, and regular checkups so small leaks are found early. If you grind your teeth, ask whether a night guard might protect your dental work.</p>

<h2>Questions to ask before a filling</h2>
<p>How large is the cavity, and can you show me on the X-ray? Is this something we could monitor instead? Which material do you recommend here, and why? What is the estimated cost, and what will my plan cover for each material? A dentist who welcomes these questions is a dentist worth keeping.</p>
""",
faq=[
("Do fillings hurt?","The area is usually numbed, so most people feel pressure and vibration rather than pain. Some sensitivity for a few days afterwards is common."),
("Can a cavity heal on its own?","Very early enamel demineralisation can sometimes be reversed with fluoride and better habits. Once a true cavity has formed, it does not grow back and usually needs treatment."),
("Should I replace my old silver fillings?","Intact amalgam fillings usually do not need replacing only because of the material. Discuss your specific situation with your dentist."),
("When can I eat after a filling?","Wait until numbness wears off to avoid biting your cheek. Composite is set immediately; with amalgam, avoid hard chewing on that side for the rest of the day."),
],
related=["dental-crowns-explained","root-canal-treatment-explained","understanding-dental-costs"],
))

A.append(dict(
slug="dental-crowns-explained",
title="Dental Crowns Explained: When You Need One and What to Expect",
tag="Procedures",
desc="Why dentists recommend crowns, the main crown materials, the step-by-step process from preparation to cementing, temporary crowns, and how to care for a crown.",
blurb="A cap for a tooth that needs more than a filling. Materials, visits and aftercare, explained plainly.",
alt="A tooth-shaped crown floating above a prepared tooth stump, with small sparkles and a dental impression tray",
body="""
<p>A crown is a cap that covers the whole visible part of a tooth above the gumline. It restores the shape, strength and appearance of a tooth that is too damaged or weakened to be fixed with a filling alone. If your dentist has recommended one, you probably have questions about why, what it will be made of, and what the process looks like. This guide covers the essentials.</p>

<h2>Why a crown might be recommended</h2>
<p>Crowns are used when a tooth needs protection all the way around rather than a patch in one place. Common reasons include:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>A large filling.</strong> When most of a tooth is already filling material, the remaining walls can crack under chewing forces.</li>
<li><strong>A cracked tooth.</strong> A crown can hold a cracked tooth together and reduce flexing, depending on where the crack runs.</li>
<li><strong>After a root canal.</strong> Back teeth that have had root canal treatment are commonly crowned because they often have lost significant structure.</li>
<li><strong>A broken or badly worn tooth.</strong> From grinding, erosion or an accident.</li>
<li><strong>Covering an implant.</strong> The visible tooth on top of a dental implant is a crown.</li>
<li><strong>Cosmetic reasons.</strong> Occasionally, for a badly discoloured or misshapen tooth, though less invasive options such as bonding or veneers may be considered first.</li>
</ul>
<p>It is fair to ask why a crown is better than a filling or onlay for your particular tooth. Crowns require removing a layer of tooth all the way around, so they are not a minor decision. A good answer will point to specific evidence, such as a crack line, the size of the existing filling, or a missing cusp.</p>

<h2>Crown materials</h2>
<figure class="table-scroll">
<table>
<caption>Main crown material families</caption>
<thead><tr><th>Material</th><th>Appearance</th><th>Often used for</th><th>Trade-offs</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Zirconia</td><td>Tooth-coloured; ranges from very strong and opaque to more translucent</td><td>Back teeth and increasingly front teeth</td><td>Very strong; highly translucent versions trade some strength for looks</td></tr>
<tr><td>Lithium disilicate and other glass ceramics</td><td>Natural translucency</td><td>Front teeth and premolars</td><td>Excellent aesthetics; bonded to the tooth</td></tr>
<tr><td>Porcelain fused to metal</td><td>Tooth-coloured outside, metal inside</td><td>Anywhere; long track record</td><td>A dark line can show at the gumline if gums recede; porcelain can chip</td></tr>
<tr><td>Gold and other metal alloys</td><td>Metallic</td><td>Back teeth with heavy bite forces</td><td>Very durable and kind to opposing teeth; less popular for looks</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<p>Your dentist will factor in which tooth it is, how much space there is, whether you grind, how visible the tooth is when you smile, and cost. Material choices can affect insurance coverage, so ask for an estimate for the option recommended.</p>

<h2>The process, step by step</h2>
<p><strong>1. Examination and planning.</strong> The dentist checks the tooth and usually takes an X-ray to make sure the root and surrounding bone are healthy. If there is deep decay or infection, that must be dealt with first, which may mean a root canal.</p>
<p><strong>2. Preparing the tooth.</strong> After numbing, the dentist removes decay and old filling material, then reduces the outer surface of the tooth evenly so the crown will fit over it without being bulky. If too little tooth remains, a "core buildup" of filling material may be placed to create a solid foundation.</p>
<p><strong>3. Taking an impression or scan.</strong> A mould is taken with impression material in a tray, or the tooth is scanned with a small digital camera. This records the exact shape so the crown fits precisely. The shade of neighbouring teeth is matched for tooth-coloured crowns.</p>
<p><strong>4a. Same-day crowns.</strong> Some practices have in-office milling machines that carve a ceramic crown from a block while you wait, allowing the whole process in one longer visit.</p>
<p><strong>4b. Temporary crown.</strong> Otherwise, a temporary crown made of acrylic or composite is cemented with a weaker cement while a dental laboratory makes the final crown, which commonly takes a couple of weeks.</p>
<p><strong>5. Fitting the final crown.</strong> The temporary is removed, and the dentist checks the fit, contacts with neighbouring teeth, bite and colour before permanently cementing or bonding the crown.</p>

<h2>Living with a temporary crown</h2>
<ul class="checklist">
<li>Chew mostly on the other side, and avoid sticky foods such as caramel and chewing gum.</li>
<li>When flossing, slide the floss out sideways rather than pulling it up, so you do not lift the temporary.</li>
<li>If it comes off, keep it, and call your dentist; the prepared tooth can be sensitive and can shift if left uncovered.</li>
<li>Some sensitivity to temperature is normal while the temporary is on.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Caring for your crown</h2>
<p>A crown itself cannot decay, but the tooth underneath can, especially at the edge where the crown meets the tooth. That margin is where plaque likes to settle. Brush carefully along the gumline, clean between teeth daily, and keep up regular checkups so the dentist can check the margins on X-rays.</p>
<p>Crowns can last many years, but lifespan varies with the material, how well the tooth is cared for, and habits like grinding, chewing ice or opening packages with your teeth. If you grind at night, a night guard can protect both crowns and natural teeth.</p>

<h2>Warning signs after a crown</h2>
<p>A little gum tenderness and some temperature sensitivity for a few days are common. Contact your dentist if your bite feels uneven, if the crown feels loose, if you notice a sharp edge, or if you develop pain when biting, lingering pain or swelling. Occasionally the nerve in a crowned tooth becomes inflamed and needs further treatment, so persistent symptoms should be checked rather than ignored.</p>
""",
faq=[
("Does getting a crown hurt?","The tooth is numbed during preparation, so most people feel pressure rather than pain. Some tenderness and sensitivity for a few days afterwards is common."),
("How long does the whole crown process take?","Same-day crowns can be finished in one visit. Lab-made crowns usually involve two visits, often a couple of weeks apart."),
("Can a tooth with a crown still get a cavity?","Yes. Decay can form where the crown meets the natural tooth, so brushing along the gumline and cleaning between teeth still matter."),
("Is a crown always needed after a root canal?","Back teeth usually are crowned after a root canal because they bear heavy forces. Front teeth sometimes are not. Your dentist will advise based on how much tooth remains."),
],
related=["root-canal-treatment-explained","dental-fillings-explained","understanding-dental-costs"],
))

A.append(dict(
slug="root-canal-treatment-explained",
title="Root Canal Treatment Explained Without the Scary Stories",
tag="Procedures",
desc="What the pulp is, why it becomes infected, how root canal treatment works step by step, recovery expectations, and the alternatives to consider.",
blurb="What actually happens inside the tooth, why it relieves pain, and what recovery is like.",
alt="A cutaway tooth showing the pulp chamber and two root canals, with tiny files and a relieved tooth character",
body="""
<p>Few dental phrases carry a worse reputation than "root canal." Yet the treatment is designed to relieve pain, not cause it, and it lets you keep a tooth that might otherwise have to be pulled. Modern anaesthesia and techniques mean many people describe the experience as similar to having a large filling. Understanding what goes on can take much of the dread out of it.</p>

<h2>Inside a tooth: the pulp</h2>
<p>Under the hard enamel and dentin of every tooth is a space containing the pulp: soft tissue with nerves, blood vessels and connective tissue. The pulp sits in a chamber in the crown of the tooth and runs down through narrow channels in each root, called root canals. Front teeth usually have one canal; molars typically have three or more.</p>
<p>The pulp matters while a tooth is developing. In a fully formed adult tooth, the tooth can survive without it, because it is nourished by the surrounding tissues. That is what makes root canal treatment possible.</p>

<h2>Why the pulp gets inflamed or infected</h2>
<p>The pulp can become inflamed or infected when bacteria reach it or when it is injured. Common causes include:</p>
<ul>
<li>Deep decay that reaches the pulp.</li>
<li>A crack or chip that exposes the inner tooth.</li>
<li>Repeated dental work on the same tooth.</li>
<li>A blow to the tooth, even if there is no visible crack.</li>
</ul>
<p>Signs can include lingering pain after hot or cold (the ache stays for many seconds or minutes after the stimulus is gone), spontaneous throbbing pain, pain that wakes you at night, tenderness when biting, a darkened tooth, a small pimple-like bump on the gum, or swelling. Sometimes there are no symptoms, and the problem is found on an X-ray as a dark area at the root tip.</p>
<p>If left untreated, an infected pulp can lead to an abscess, a pocket of infection at the end of the root, which can spread and become serious. Facial swelling, fever or difficulty swallowing need urgent attention.</p>

<h2>What happens during root canal treatment</h2>
<p>Root canal treatment may be done by a general dentist or by an endodontist, a specialist in this area. Complex molars or retreatments are often referred to an endodontist. The steps are broadly the same:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Diagnosis.</strong> The dentist tests the tooth's response to cold and tapping and takes X-rays to see the root shapes and any infection.</li>
<li><strong>Numbing.</strong> Local anaesthetic numbs the tooth and surrounding area.</li>
<li><strong>Isolation.</strong> A rubber dam, a small sheet held by a clamp, isolates the tooth to keep it clean and dry and to stop you swallowing solutions or tiny instruments.</li>
<li><strong>Access.</strong> A small opening is made through the top of the tooth into the pulp chamber.</li>
<li><strong>Cleaning and shaping.</strong> Thin flexible files remove the pulp tissue and shape each canal, while disinfecting solutions are used to rinse it. The dentist measures canal lengths with X-rays or an electronic device.</li>
<li><strong>Filling the canals.</strong> The cleaned canals are filled and sealed, most commonly with a rubbery material called gutta-percha and a sealer.</li>
<li><strong>Sealing the top.</strong> A temporary or permanent filling closes the access opening. Treatment can be completed in one visit or spread over two, sometimes with medication placed inside the tooth between visits.</li>
</ol>

<figure class="box">
<h3>Root canal at a glance</h3>
<ul class="checklist">
<li><strong>Goal:</strong> remove infected or inflamed pulp, disinfect the canals, seal them, keep the tooth.</li>
<li><strong>Who does it:</strong> a general dentist or an endodontist.</li>
<li><strong>Numbing:</strong> local anaesthetic, as with a filling.</li>
<li><strong>Visits:</strong> commonly one or two.</li>
<li><strong>Afterwards:</strong> a final restoration, often a crown for back teeth.</li>
</ul>
</figure>

<h2>Recovery: what is normal</h2>
<p>For a few days, the tooth and gum may feel tender, especially when biting. This is usually manageable with over-the-counter pain relievers taken as directed on the label, if they are safe for you; check with your dentist or pharmacist, especially if you have medical conditions or take other medicines. Avoid chewing hard foods on that tooth until it has its final restoration, because a tooth with a temporary filling is vulnerable to breaking.</p>
<p>Contact your dentist if pain is severe or getting worse after the first few days, if swelling appears or increases, if you have a fever, if the temporary filling falls out, or if your bite feels uneven. If antibiotics were prescribed, take them exactly as instructed; note that antibiotics are not a substitute for treatment when a tooth is the source of the infection.</p>

<h2>The final restoration matters</h2>
<p>A root canal treats the inside of the tooth, but it does not rebuild the outside. Back teeth that have had root canals are usually weakened and commonly need a crown or onlay to protect them from cracking. Delaying the final restoration for a long time increases the risk of fracture or of bacteria leaking back into the canals. Plan the next step with your dentist before you leave.</p>

<h2>Alternatives to consider</h2>
<p>The main alternative to a root canal on an infected tooth is extraction. Removing the tooth ends the infection, but it leaves a gap that can affect chewing and allow neighbouring teeth to drift. Replacing a missing tooth with an implant, bridge or partial denture adds its own cost, time and trade-offs. In most cases, saving a natural tooth that has a good outlook is preferred, but the decision depends on how much healthy tooth remains, the condition of the gums and bone, and your priorities. Ask your dentist to explain the long-term outlook for the tooth and the full cost of each path, including replacement if you choose extraction.</p>

<h2>What about the myths?</h2>
<p>You may come across claims online that root canals cause illness elsewhere in the body. These ideas trace back to research from the early 1900s that later investigators could not reproduce, and major dental organisations do not support them. If you have concerns, raise them with your dentist; you deserve a clear, evidence-based answer.</p>
""",
faq=[
("Is a root canal painful?","The tooth is numbed, and many people find the procedure similar to a large filling. Tenderness for a few days afterwards is common. The treatment is intended to relieve the pain of an infected tooth."),
("How many visits does a root canal take?","Often one or two, depending on the tooth, the amount of infection and the dentist's approach. A crown or other restoration may require further visits."),
("Can I just take antibiotics instead?","Antibiotics can help control spreading infection, but they do not clean out an infected pulp. The source of infection still needs treatment, either a root canal or extraction."),
("Will the tooth feel dead afterwards?","The tooth will no longer feel hot or cold, but you will still feel pressure through the surrounding ligament and bone, so biting feels normal."),
],
related=["dental-crowns-explained","dental-emergency-guide","dental-fillings-explained"],
))
